---
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import Container from "../components/Container.astro";
import FeatureCard from "../components/FeatureCard.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import CredentialPrivacyDiagram from "../components/CredentialPrivacyDiagram.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Security — Syntila"
  description="Syntila separates Space collaboration, AI Account authorization, and Workspace execution to keep your secrets private."
>
  <Section>
    <Container>
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Security & Privacy"
        title="Share AI access without sharing your secrets."
        description="Syntila separates Space collaboration in the Syntila app and Syntila Cloud from local CLI execution on your Syntila Workspace, so collaborators can use authorized AI Workers without seeing your API keys or provider logins."
        headingLevel={1}
      />

      <div class="section-block-gap">
        <CredentialPrivacyDiagram />
      </div>

      <div class="section-block-gap-lg">
        <SectionHeader
          eyebrow="Authorization Boundaries"
          title="Strict separation between people, spaces, and AI."
          description="Every layer in Syntila enforces an explicit boundary between Space membership, Workspace grants, and local filesystem state."
        />

        <div class="security-boundaries">
          <FeatureCard title="Space Membership" headingLevel={2}>
            <p>Defines who can view Threads (<strong>Viewer</strong>), participate and run authorized AI Workers (<strong>Collaborator</strong>), or manage members and Space grants (<strong>Owner</strong>). Joining a Space never grants direct access to anyone's personal AI accounts or machines.</p>
          </FeatureCard>

          <FeatureCard title="AI Account Privacy" headingLevel={2}>
            <p>Provider credentials remain in the local CLI environment on the owner's Workspace host. Granting a Worker to a Space authorizes eligible members to submit requests through Syntila; it never transmits the underlying API key or session token to client apps or devices.</p>
          </FeatureCard>

          <FeatureCard title="Workspace Isolation" headingLevel={2}>
            <p>Each Space runs inside its own Space Work Directory under the Workspace's configured Work Root, with optional Allowed Folders per Space and exclusive Thread mutation leases to serialize file-modifying steps.</p>
          </FeatureCard>

          <FeatureCard title="Audit & Attribution" headingLevel={2}>
            <p>The Workspace Service verifies Ed25519 signatures on Worker Profiles before executing provider CLIs, and execution records attribute every run to the requesting user, Workspace, Worker, and model.</p>
          </FeatureCard>

          <FeatureCard title="MCP Tool Pinning &amp; Consent" headingLevel={2}>
            <p>Every local or remote MCP server requires explicit owner consent before use. Syntila pins SHA-256 hashes of tool definitions, blocks execution on definition drift, and keeps server tokens and environment variables local to the Workspace.</p>
          </FeatureCard>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="security-callout">
        <strong>Revocable Space grants without credential rotation.</strong>
        <span>Revoking a Workspace or Worker grant—or removing a member from a Space—stops Syntila Cloud from admitting new assignments for that Space or member without requiring you to rotate local provider API keys or logins.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="actions actions--center section-block-gap-lg">
        <Button href="https://syntila.app" label="Open Syntila" event="cta.open_app" />
        <Button href="/privacy/" label="Read privacy policy" variant="secondary" />
        <Button href="/terms/" label="Read terms of service" variant="secondary" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</SiteLayout>
